myanmar's military has been  systematically torturing detainees in  the wake of pro-democracy protests in  the country that's according to an  investigation by the ap news agency  based on interviews with former  prisoners and people who worked in the  army the junta has arrested more than 7  000 people since a coup overthrew the  democratically elected government of  nobel laureate aung sangsuchi in  february this year and despite  international sanctions the junta's use  of torture to suppress democracy  continues  march 2021  people in yangon still had faith they  could reverse the month-long military  takeover  those hopes were crushed as the junta  moved in and swept up hundreds of  fleeing protesters loading them into  trucks  since then it's happened over and over  again leaving physical and mental scars  for the detainees  [Music]  nathan hmong was one of them  now back in the u.s the journalist  described his experience in the  interrogation center  he's like holding one of this you know  bamboo stick or something like you don't  know but he hitting me like this  it's like two hours and the guy was  standing behind me  start hitting  like  [ __ ]  dog  these images obtained by ap shows wounds  of former detainees they say it's  evidence of torture  they say protesters including women and  children were held in centers like these  the military dismisses the claims as  nonsense  but army defector hinyang pyong says he  witnessed the suffering with his own  eyes  since the start of the demonstrations  the police and army arrested beat  tortured people  they did it to everyone who were  arrested  i saw that two people died from their  physical torture  to improve its image the junta released  more than a thousand of around five  thousand detainees in october  but over a hundred of them were  re-arrested  activists call it mental torture in  addition to the physical punishment  endured in jail  human rights organizations say in  myanmar this is nothing  them using this new the religion ethnic  minority groups  um we've seen them using this against  the other ethnic minority groups for  decades and now they're employing it  against civilian people  now a free man hmong says he's finding  some solace in buddhist prayer but the  memories continue to haunt him  especially knowing thousands of others  remain behind bars  and joining me now from bangkok is phil  robertson he's deputy director of the  asia division at human rights watch phil  are you actually surprised to hear about  these reports of prisoners being  tortured in myanmar  no i'm not and that's kind of a sad  commentary because we have seen  systematic use of torture by military  governments in in burma in the past  and to see again this happening and to  be  reported by ap in such a  very conclusive fashion  really shows that it's a reversion to  past practice that with the military  coup they have brought in all their bad  human rights practices uh and again the  impunity that allows them to go forward  with this i mean no one is ever  prosecuted in the myanmar military for  use of torture  and uh this is why it continues to  grow and to be used systematically but  how are they getting away with it in the  21st century i mean how is the  international community  in a sense allowing this to happen  well it's an excellent question i think  that uh because we have not been able to  get traction on uh burma at the u.n  security council because of the  resistance of both russia and china uh  the whole accountability effort to  bring these uh various  perpetrators to justice  not only for what they're doing now to  the burmese people but also what they've  done in the past to  the ethnic rohingya in 2017 and to other  ethnic minorities all over burma that we  you know are  are really stymied in our ability to get  international accountability and  certainly at the national level in burma  uh the courts are beholden to the  military and will do nothing to  challenge them short of the u.n the eu  and the u.s have for instance already  import sanctions on members of the  myanmar junta but clearly that doesn't  seem to be  making an impact is it time therefore to  step up the pressure with newer and more  targeted sanctions i mean do we need to  look at the oil and gas industries  in myanmar for instance  well we are looking very closely at the  oil and gas  money that goes to the burmese junta you  know it's well over 400 million dollars  a year and that needs to be  cut off we have a lot of  proposals for that to stop the transfers  of money into  accounts controlled by the burmese  military but so far we've met a lot of  resistance particularly by the  government of france but also by other  states within the european union  it is unfortunate that we're not seeing  the kind of sanctioning that we need to  see and while we're hearing about a  possible new round of sanctions that may  be coming forward in the european union  uh it's it's again too little too late  it's been it's been  almost nine months now uh since we have  seen this coup and and what has happened  here uh people are dying every day  people are being arrested on mass people  are being tortured and the international  community can only come up with  statements  of condemnation  it's made the burmese people very  cynical and uh they're demanding more  action  does that basically mean that the people  of myanmar have been left to their own  devices they are left with their own  fate to try and stand up to this junta  to try and restore democracy in their  own country  well  certainly the burmese people think so uh  and that's what they're saying if you  watch what's happening on social media  and you listen to what they're telling  uh the few reporters who are still  active in the country is that it's on  them it's up to them to make something  happen and this is why i think we've  seen uh you know in a course of  desperation uh people taking up arms and  the growth o